Seeing all four modifiers in one class makes the spectrum concrete. Each field below carries the narrowest access it can tolerate, and the methods expose only what the outside world legitimately needs.

```java
package com.company.model;

public class BankAccount {
    private double balance;       // only BankAccount can access
    String accountNumber;          // default: same package only
    protected String ownerName;    // same package + subclasses anywhere
    public String bankName;        // accessible everywhere

    public void deposit(double amount) {  // public API
        if (amount > 0) balance += amount; // only this class touches balance
    }

    protected double getBalance() {       // subclasses can call this
        return balance;
    }
}
```

Notice the logic behind each choice: `balance` is the most sensitive value so it is `private`, while `bankName` is shared freely as `public`. The modifier on every line is really a one-word statement of who is allowed to depend on that member.

```java
public class User {
    private int age;
    public void setAge(int age) { this.age = age; } // no checks — barely better than public
}
```

```java
public class User {
    private int age;
    public void setAge(int age) {
        if (age < 0 || age > 150)
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Age must be 0-150");
        this.age = age; // only valid values reach the field
    }
}
```
The validation is the whole point of a setter — without it, a private field plus a pass-through setter is effectively public with extra typing.

A realistic accessor does two jobs at once — it guards the value and it cleans it up. This `Account` rejects blank usernames and malformed emails, and it also normalizes whatever it keeps.

```java
public class Account {
    private String username;
    private String email;

    public String getUsername() { return username; }

    public void setUsername(String username) {
        if (username == null || username.isBlank())
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Username required");
        this.username = username.trim();      // normalize
    }

    public void setEmail(String email) {
        if (!email.contains("@"))
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid email");
        this.email = email.toLowerCase();     // normalize
    }
}
```

Because callers can never write the field directly, every value that lands in `username` or `email` is already trimmed and lower-cased. That guarantee is only possible when the field is private and the setter is the single doorway in.

```java
// package com.company.model
package com.company.model;
public class Animal {
    void breathe() {}        // default (package-private)
    protected void eat() {}  // protected
}
```

```java
// package com.company.pets — a DIFFERENT package
package com.company.pets;
import com.company.model.Animal;

class Dog extends Animal {
    void test() {
        eat();       // OK — protected crosses packages via inheritance
        // breathe(); // COMPILE ERROR — default is blocked across packages
    }
}
```
The single line that compiles versus the one that doesn't is the whole interview answer: `eat()` is reachable because `protected` follows the inheritance link across packages, while `breathe()` is not because default never leaves its own package.
